# User study: protocol and statistics

// ## Protocol
// 
// - **Platform:** Amazon Mechanical Turk, two batches, 120 assignments (one assignment per HIT).
// - **Raters:** 120 assignments from 116 unique workers.
// - **Task:** each assignment showed 9 pairs of videos (ours vs. one baseline) with audio, each pair showing front and side views via a circular camera path. For each pair, raters chose the better video for three criteria: lip-sync accuracy, video realism and image quality (forced binary choice, no tie option). This gives 27 judgments per assignment and 3,240 in total.
// - **Design:** 6 baselines × 9 subjects × 2 audio clips, each pair rated by 10 assignments: 180 judgments per baseline and criterion.
// - **Counterbalancing:** our result appeared on the left in exactly 50% of pairs; raters chose the left video 52.3% of the time, so the slight position bias cancels out.
// - **Time:** median 13.7 min per assignment (interquartile range 8.4–20.5 min); 30-minute limit.

// ## Results

// Each cell: % of judgments preferring the baseline over ours [95% CI] / p (two-sided binomial test against 50%). n = 180 judgments per cell; lower % is better for ours. Confidence intervals use the normal approximation, p̂ ± 1.96·√(p̂(1−p̂)/n).


